# Start one external and one internal job in the background, then use bare `fg` twice
# to bring each one to the foreground in turn, confirming control genuinely moves
# between two distinct jobs.
#
# This does NOT rely on each job's own side-effect output (e.g. an `echo` inside the
# backgrounded job itself) to prove which job ran -- a background job's own output is
# printed by the global Printer thread the instant that job finishes on its own
# schedule, completely independent of whether/when it's ever brought to the
# foreground: `&` only skips *waiting* for a job's result, it does not gate *when*
# the job's output appears on `fg` ever being called. Confirmed directly: an earlier
# version of this test used exactly that approach (each background job printing its
# own distinct marker), and both markers appeared even with the `fg` calls removed
# entirely, and even with the two expected markers swapped to the "wrong" order --
# i.e. it never actually exercised `fg`'s job-selection behavior at all, just two
# jobs finishing on their own schedule.
#
# Instead, each job's distinct marker is its own *return value* -- the value `&`
# registers for later retrieval via `fg` (see `Job::eval`'s `is_background` branch and
# `GlobalState::add_background_job`/`take_last_background_job` in
# src/lang/state/global_state.rs) -- and that value only ever reaches the terminal by
# being explicitly captured from `fg`'s own return and echoed (`convert $string
# $(fg)`), which is possible only once `fg` has actually resumed that specific job and
# received its result.
#
# For the external job, a bare `cmd ... &` (with no further pipeline stage) gets the
# real tty handed to it directly (see `cmd_internal`'s `use_tty` check in
# src/builtins/control/cmd.rs) -- its child process's stdout bypasses crush's own
# value pipes entirely, and the background job's registered result is just `Value::
# Empty`. Piping it into `convert $string` (whose own context.output is a real pipe,
# forcing `use_tty` to false) makes `cmd` capture the child's stdout into a
# `binary_stream` instead and forwards it downstream, so the *pipeline's* result --
# what `fg` actually retrieves -- is the command's own captured output converted to a
# string. Confirmed directly against the debug binary.
#
# For the internal job, a bare `{...} &` block's own result is simply the value its
# last statement evaluates to -- no piping needed.
#
# Both markers are built from two separate pieces joined via `:format`/`printf`
# (`"{}_{}":format "INTERNAL" "MARKER"`, `printf '%s_%s' EXTERNAL MARKER`) rather than
# written as one contiguous literal (`"INTERNAL_MARKER"`) -- `pty`'s pty is a real
# terminal, and the command *line* that starts each background job is itself echoed
# onto the rendered screen by the pty's own line discipline the instant it's typed,
# well before the job has actually produced anything. A one-piece literal marker
# would make that typed-but-not-yet-run echo of the starting command contain the
# exact same substring `wait_for` checks for later, making the check pass
# regardless of whether `fg` ever ran -- confirmed directly: an earlier version of
# this test (before this comment) used one-piece literals and passed even with the
# `fg` calls removed and with the expected markers swapped, for exactly this reason.
# Splitting each marker into two pieces joined only once the job actually runs means
# the full joined string never appears anywhere on screen until that job's result is
# actually captured and echoed.
#
# Bare `fg` (no job id) operates on the *most recently* backgrounded job -- a LIFO
# stack, see `GlobalState::take_last_background_job` (`background_jobs` is a
# `Vec`, popped from the back). Since the internal job is started second here, the
# first `fg` resumes it, and the second `fg` resumes the external one. Confirmed
# directly against the debug binary.
#
# Uses `pty_run`'s counted prompt (see pty_test_helpers.crush) instead of a bare
# `wait_for "# "` plus a settle delay: a bare `wait_for "# "` can be satisfied by a
# prompt already on screen *before* the write, so it proves nothing about whether the
# command just written has actually been submitted, let alone finished -- which is
# exactly why the external `cmd ... | convert $string &` pipeline here used to need a
# full 1s settle delay (a real fork/exec plus a second pipeline stage genuinely can
# take that long to even get started) while a plain builtin needed only 300ms, and
# guessing wrong in either direction either slowed every run down for the worst case
# or corrupted the rendered screen under load. A counted prompt has no such tradeoff:
# `pty_run` returns the instant (and not before) the right prompt is genuinely
# rendered, however long that actually took.
source tests/pty_test_helpers.crush
$t := $(pty_spawn)
$n := $(pty_enable_counted_prompt $t)

$n = $(pty_run $t $n "cmd sh -c 'sleep 1; printf \"%s_%s\" EXTERNAL MARKER' | convert $string &")
$n = $(pty_run $t $n "{sleep 1s; \"{}_{}\":format \"INTERNAL\" \"MARKER\"} &")

$n = $(pty_run $t $n "echo $(convert $string $(fg))")
$t:wait_for "INTERNAL_MARKER"

$n = $(pty_run $t $n "echo $(convert $string $(fg))")
$t:wait_for "EXTERNAL_MARKER"

$t:close
